WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.446
Login endpoints need protection against repeated failed attempts and automated abuse.

2
00:00:05.446 --> 00:00:11.618
A password system should not allow unlimited verification attempts without visibility or control.

3
00:00:11.618 --> 00:00:20.984
At the same time, the controls should avoid creating unnecessary denial of service, support burden, or account recovery friction for legitimate users.

4
00:00:20.984 --> 00:00:24.107
Lockout and throttling policies require balance.

5
00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:31.368
Account-based limits can protect a specific user, but they can also be triggered in ways that inconvenience that user.

6
00:00:31.368 --> 00:00:38.338
IP-based limits can reduce high-volume noise, but they may affect shared networks or miss distributed behavior.

7
00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:47.052
Tenant, device signal, endpoint, and risk-based controls may be useful depending on the application architecture and available telemetry.

8
00:00:47.052 --> 00:00:54.966
Credential stuffing should be understood as a defensive risk pattern: many reused credentials are attempted against accounts.

9
00:00:54.966 --> 00:01:12.248
The safe engineering response is not to study operational abuse methods, but to monitor for unusual failure rates, password reset spikes, unfamiliar locations, impossible travel signals, locked accounts, and other indicators that the authentication surface is under pressure.

10
00:01:12.248 --> 00:01:17.621
Error messages and timing should not intentionally reveal unnecessary account state.

11
00:01:17.621 --> 00:01:24.882
The client experience should remain predictable while the server records enough detail for monitoring and response.

12
00:01:24.882 --> 00:01:32.797
The application can provide user-friendly recovery options without turning every failure into a detailed diagnostic message.

13
00:01:32.797 --> 00:01:36.427
Controls should be measurable and tuneable in production.

14
00:01:36.427 --> 00:01:44.705
Teams should know how many failures are normal, which alerts matter, when support should be involved, and how exceptions are approved.

15
00:01:44.705 --> 00:01:51.530
Rate limiting is most useful when it is observable, tested, documented, and adjusted as the application grows.
